    "lawCode": "Amd §35-0105, En Con L",
    "lawSection": "Environmental Conservation Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S6603\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\n\nAn act to amend the environmental conservation law, in relation to\nprohibiting household cleansing products that contain 1, 4-dioxane\n\n PURPOSE :\n\nThe purpose of this bill is to improve water quality and public health\nby eliminating the use of 1, 4-dioxane in household cleaning products.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection one amends § 35-0105 of the Environmental Conservation Law by\nadding a new subdivision 4 to prohibit the distribution, selling,\noffering and exposure of household cleaning products that contain more\nthan trace concentrations of 1, 4- dioxane.\n\nSection two provides an effective date of April 1, 2019.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nThe chemical 1,4-dioxane has been designated as a likely carcinogen by\nthe United States Environmental Protection Agency. The chemical is not\nadded to products intentionally, but is instead the product of a\nprocess called ethoxylation, which is used to reduce the risk of skin\nirritation in consumer products. The chemical can be removed from\nproducts through processes including vacuum stripping but often\nmanufacturers are unwilling to incur the added expense. Laundry\ndetergents often have the highest chemical concentrations. This is\nproblematic because 1,4-dioxane has become an increasingly common\nwater contaminant, especially on Long Island. According to a February\n24th Newsday article, test results \"showed that 71 percent of water\nsuppliers tested on Long Island had concentrations of 1,4-dioxane that\npose a cancer risk after prolonged exposure. Nationwide, 7 percent of\nwater suppliers detected similar cancer risk concentrations.\" This\nbill would limit the amount of 1,4-dioxane that could be contained in\ncleaning products.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nNew Bill\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nNone.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nApril 1, 2019\n",
